Daily read-and-review processing pass for the protocols-research stream.

## Working hypothesis (the lens for this digest)

The digest exists to gather **evidence and citations** for the project's fundamental hypothesis. Authoritative framing lives in [STATEMENT.md](STATEMENT.md); the working frame is that human–AI collaboration is the externalisation of human intent-making into a non-human system that can act on it (as supplement, replacement, or to enable transhuman work humans cannot do alone), and that protocols are the mechanism for monitoring, refining, and validating that work along its trajectory to fulfilling intent.

> Transferring human-derived protocols into transhuman (human + AI) protocols is the best path forward.

Three pillars structure the digest. Pillars A and B are the hypothesis's sub-claims. Pillar C is the design-property lens drawn from STATEMENT.md — recurring properties of effective protocols across human and transhuman traditions. Score and surface highlights against the specific sub-claims under each.

### Pillar A — Humans need to be in the loop

- A1. Humans themselves often lack clarity, so the loop is where intent gets refined.
- A2. AI lacks intentionality.
- A3. AI lacks causal understanding (no genuine world models).
- A4. AI hallucinates.

### Pillar B — Protocols for effective human collaboration extend to transhuman collaboration

- B1. AI was trained on human data, so human-coordination patterns are already encoded in its priors.
- B2. Protocols and their results need to be inspectable (predicated on Pillar A).
- B3. Human constraints (cognition, attention, time, energy) are a limiting factor that protocols must respect.
- B4. Humans hold context that AI will never have without the human supplying it.
- B5. Humans are the final arbiter of success or failure.
- B6. Humans will be accountable to other humans in existential ways (legal, moral, reputational, civic).

### Pillar C — Design properties of effective protocols (from STATEMENT.md)

Properties that recur across human collaboration traditions (GTD, agile, pair programming, etc.) and transfer, sometimes with modification, into transhuman collaboration. Use these as a parallel scoring lens — articles that exemplify, sharpen, or critique any C-property are first-class fit even when they don't map cleanly to A/B.

- C1. Data gathering — what gets captured and at what fidelity.
- C2. Clarification practices — how ambiguity gets surfaced and resolved.
- C3. Inspectability and monitoring — how the protocol's state and results can be examined (overlaps with B2; expected — same evidence may surface under both).
- C4. Accountability — roles and domains of influence; who answers for what.
- C5. Shared context at multiple horizons — how context is preserved, propagated, and time-scoped (turn, session, project, programme).
- C6. Standards for language, tools, and behaviours — conventions that make the protocol legible and repeatable.

### Effectiveness definition (the scoring axis)

Effectiveness is judged on two layers, drawn from STATEMENT.md. Use both when labelling evidence:

- **Layer 1 — outputs vs intent.** Did the protocol produce what was intended?
- **Layer 2 — wider implications.** Individual: cognitive load, cognitive sovereignty, cognitive debt. Group: governance, bias, social impact.

Articles that bear on Layer 2 (e.g. cognitive-surrender studies, governance frameworks, bias mechanisms) are first-class evidence — surface them with the same weight as Layer 1 results.

Treat counter-evidence (claims that weaken any of A1–B6 or any C-property, or that complicate the effectiveness definition) as equally valuable — flag it explicitly so the framework can be sharpened or revised.

## Inputs

- `/Users/rpeake/Desktop/project-support/00-protocols/STATEMENT.md` — authoritative project framing (definition of human–AI collaboration, the six design properties, the effectiveness definition). Re-read each run; if it has changed materially, note that in the top-line framing.
- `/Users/rpeake/Desktop/project-support/00-protocols/read-and-review/` — articles awaiting review. Reading rules:
  - `tier: high` — read in full.
  - `tier: medium` — read in full where slug/source maps to A1–B6, to a C-property, to the effectiveness Layer 2 lens (cognitive load/sovereignty/debt; governance/bias/social impact), or to considerations.opml.
  - `tier: low` — frontmatter `reason` + `## Summary` only; escalate to full read on strong fit against any of the lenses above.
- `/Users/rpeake/Desktop/project-support/00-protocols/considerations.opml` — broader theme tree. Use as secondary mapping; the A/B/C grid is primary.
- `/Users/rpeake/Desktop/project-support/00-protocols/CONVENTIONS.md` — read-and-review file shape and read/ migration rules.

## Output 1 — Email digest to robert@peakepro.com

Create a Gmail draft (subject `read-and-review digest — YYYY-MM-DD`) structured around the hypothesis, not around source-of-the-week. Body:

1. **Top-line framing** (3–5 sentences): which sub-claims (A/B) and design properties (C) gained the most evidentiary weight today, which weakened, any cross-cutting threads, and any Layer 2 effectiveness signal worth flagging.
2. **Evidence sections**, one per sub-claim or C-property that picked up notable signal. Use the labels (A1–B6, C1–C6) as section headers, with the claim or property restated. Under each, 1–4 bullets where each bullet is:
   - the specific finding/argument (one sentence — the actual claim, not a generic gloss),
   - whether it **supports** or **complicates / contradicts** the sub-claim/property,
   - where applicable, which effectiveness layer it speaks to (Layer 1 / Layer 2),
   - a citation: `— [source-slug](read/<filename>.md)`. Use the full filename with date prefix so the link resolves after the move.
   - Where the source contains a sharp quotable phrase, include it inline in quotes.
3. **Cross-cutting / framework moves** — anything that suggests adding, splitting, or retiring a sub-claim or C-property, that bridges pillars usefully, or that sharpens the effectiveness definition. Optional; only if real signal.
4. **Mapping** — short list (5–12 lines) of which considerations.opml branches and which C-properties got fed today, so the broader framework stays visible. One line per branch or property: `Branch/property name — N items, strongest = [source-slug]`.
5. **Skip list** — items considered and dismissed (filename + 4-word reason, max 8). Only include obvious skips worth flagging.

Tone: trusted-advisor, dense, no padding, no emojis. Pitch to a technical generalist familiar with the framework — assume Robert knows his own pillars and design properties. Length target 700–1200 words; let signal set the actual length.

## Output 2 — File migration

After drafting the email:

- Move every dated article (everything except `_TEMPLATE.md`) from `read-and-review/` to `read/`.
- Update each moved file's frontmatter in place: `status: read-and-review` → `status: read`, `read_date: null` → `read_date: <today ISO date>`.
- Leave `_TEMPLATE.md` in `read-and-review/`.
- Verify zero residual `status: read-and-review` or `read_date: null` strings in `read/`.

## Operating notes

- "Email me" = create a Gmail draft. Do not auto-send unless a send capability is exposed and the task explicitly says to send.
- Run autonomously — Robert is not present. Make reasonable choices and note them in the digest's top-line framing if they affected coverage.
- When evidence is thin, say so. Don't pad sections to look complete. A digest that names the gap is more useful than one that papers over it.
- Counter-evidence is first-class output — surface it under the same sub-claim/property header with "complicates / contradicts" labelling.
- Same evidence may legitimately appear under both an A/B sub-claim and a C-property (e.g. activation-introspection results under B2 and C3). Don't deduplicate at the cost of clarity; do cross-reference where it helps.
